@@ -48,11 +76,12 @@ Other points:
 | 1.1 | The rule matches a classic *transformed up-down* staircase. See 1.2. | 🟡 |
 | 1.2 | **Confirmed in the literature.** A rule that makes the level go up after two correct and down after one miss converges on about **70.7% correct** (Levitt 1971). So a student settles where they answer roughly 7 in 10 correctly — higher than a "50/50" level and not the same as "mastered". Sources: [Levitt 1971 summary](https://typeset.io/papers/transformed-up-down-methods-in-psychoacoustics-31x0jr24ab), [lecture notes](https://engineering.purdue.edu/~ece511/LectureNotes/pp12.pdf). The app's docs should say so. | 🟢 |
 | 1.3 | Levitt-style methods estimate the threshold from the **mean of the reversal levels**; the app uses the **final level** instead. No rule on how many reversals or items are enough appeared in the snippets (check Levitt's paper). | 🟡 |
-| 1.4 | No accuracy data can exist without your own pilot. See "Studies you can run" on the agenda. | ⚪ |
+| 1.4 | No independent study of this app's estimate can exist without your pilot. For other placement tests, validity studies report agreement with expert raters using weighted kappa (e.g. [TrackTest](https://tracktest.eu/wp-content/uploads/2022/11/TrackTestValidityStudy-expert-agreement.pdf)); most accuracy claims found are vendor marketing. Use kappa and exact/±1 agreement in your own pilot. | 🟡 |
 | 1.5–1.7, 1.10–1.12 | Nothing specific found; these need your own item data and expert review. | ⚪ |
 | 1.6 | Dutch school levels differ (A2 expected for havo/vwo year 1–2 vs A1 for vmbo-bb), which supports starting at a level that follows the class — see 4.1. | 🟡 |
 | 1.8 | Pelánek (2016) is the standard review of Elo in education and notes the usual improvement of an **uncertainty function** (smaller steps as an item gathers responses) instead of a fixed K ([paper](https://www.fi.muni.cz/~xpelanek/publications/CAE-elo.pdf)). The app uses a fixed K = 24. A 2026 follow-up says shrinking-K schemes are inadequate in some situations. | 🟢 |
 | 1.9 | Nothing specific found on cut-offs. | ⚪ |
+| 1.9 | MST research says **misrouting falls with longer tests and a routing module spanning a wide ability range**; adding stages helps only modestly. Routing-rule errors and weak routing items are named as causes. No guidance on the percentage cut-off itself. See [overview](https://core.ac.uk/download/345079362.pdf). | 🟡 |
 
