+| 2.3 | Dutch practice: marks run 1–10; Cito advises an **absolute** norm per test (decide the pass score beforehand) with a relative norm only as a supplement; the common conversion gives **6.0 at the cesuur** and spreads 6.0–10.0 **linearly** up to the maximum score; central exams use the N-term. See [Cito normeringstypen](https://cito.nl/media/5kchoyu2/normeringstypen-absolute-en-relatieve-normering_v2.pdf), [toets.nl handleiding](https://handleiding.toets.nl/p/export?id=1063). The app's default grade scales have not yet been compared against this. | 🟡 |
+| 2.6 | Evidence on late penalties is small-scale: a penalty reduces late work but doesn't make more students submit on time; the best results in one study came from an early bonus plus a late penalty; critics argue heavy penalties hurt struggling students; short grace periods are a common compromise. See [ASEE study](https://peer.asee.org/impact-of-late-policies-on-submission-behavior-and-grades-in-computer-programming). The app's free-form modifier with a stored reason fits this. | 🟡 |
+| 2.10 | A meta-analysis of grading experiments (Malouff & Thorsteinsson 2016; 20 studies, 1,935 graders) found **bias when graders know irrelevant student information (g = 0.36)**, at all school levels. Halo effects between criteria are also reported, and a **random presentation order of criteria reduced the group-level halo**. No evidence found specifically on handwriting. See [meta-analysis](https://rune.une.edu.au/web/handle/1959.11/19701). | 🟢 |
 
 ## 3. Question types & scoring
 
@@ -70,7 +102,10 @@ Other points:
 |---|---|---|
 | 3.1 | Studies on multiple-response scoring generally find **partial-credit rules perform better psychometrically than all-or-nothing**, including Ripkey's rule and balanced rules; the differences are sometimes small. The app's rule (credit for correctly *unselected* options too) is a form of "balanced" scoring but gives a non-zero score to a blank answer — compare it with other rules. See [Psychometrika framework](https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/psychometrika/article/psychometric-model-framework-for-multiple-response-items/10D6DCD514D67281E4695FFC00C88B6A) and the 2021 EPM paper "Evaluating Different Scoring Methods for Multiple Response Items Providing Partial Credit" (DOI 10.1177/0013164421994636). | 🟢 |
 | 3.6 | The **C-test** (damaged words) is designed around "reduced redundancy" and general proficiency; the **rational-deletion cloze** gives the author control over *content* but is *not* a measure of general proficiency (Grotjahn, quoted). Fixed-ratio cloze results depend on text, deletion rate and starting point. Construct validity of the C-test is still debated, and one study suggests it also reflects processing speed ([overview](https://research.bangor.ac.uk/portal/files/78069213/28Jul2020The_C-test_as_predictor_final_manuscript.pdf)). The app's gap generators should be labelled accordingly. | 🟢 |
-| 3.2–3.5, 3.7–3.11 | Not searched in this pass. | ⚪ |
+| 3.3, 3.5, 3.7, 3.8, 3.10, 3.11 | Not found yet; 3.8 needs your own audit. | ⚪ |
+| 3.2 | **Correction for guessing** (formula scoring) reduces guessing noise, but is criticised for adding anxiety and for **risk-attitude effects**; findings on gender bias are inconsistent and depend on how the rule is framed. Alternatives include elimination scoring. See [Sussex analysis](https://blogs.sussex.ac.uk/learning-matters/2024/06/11/guessing-and-gender-bias-in-multiple-choice-quizzes-with-negative-marking/). The app doesn't apply one; more items is the simpler fix. | 🟡 |
+| 3.4 | Dictation is usually scored at word level, with leeway for spelling; research suggests **combining word-, letter- and sound-level measures**, and criticises dictation because it is hard to tell a listening error from a spelling error. See [measures paper](https://forms.mpi.nl/node/54179). | 🟡 |
+| 3.9 | Common classroom guidelines: discrimination **≥ 0.40 very good, 0.30–0.39 good, 0.20–0.29 fair, < 0.20 weak, negative = check the answer key** (Ebel-style; based on practice more than hard evidence). The app already colours discrimination below 0.30 as a warning. See [UT Austin guide](https://testingservices.utexas.edu/interpreting-test-results). | 🟢 |
 
